    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The corners are dominated by figures clad in layers of tweed, oversized parkas, or heavy denim that often obscure what they're hiding, their faces etched with a permanent, guarded weariness. You'll see \"hangers-on\" who act as محیط sensors— veterans who know every dead end and recent violent altercation—sitting on cracked ledges or sprawled against graffiti-scraped chain-link fences, sharpening cans or staring at phone screens with a deep, unblinking suspicion. These are the locals, the living texture of the zone: men who move with a slow, coiled tension, trading low, hushed slang over cigarettes that smoke their fingers, turning their bodies away from the protagonist like solid steel walls until the moment they decide to become part of the story.\n\nEvery individual seems to carry a heavy history, a look in their eyes that has seen too much violence without sparing judgment, creating a wall of silent, watchful absence where your character is constantly evaluated. They are not just background noise; they are the living decay, a collection of survivors whose presence alone makes the air feel thicker, making the protagonist feel small and constantly rehearsed in the art of not connecting.",
